# Input text direction (first letter)

Free-text **inputs** and **textareas** use `resolveInputDir()` / `inputTextDir()` from [`utils/inputAutoDir.ts`](utils/inputAutoDir.ts): **empty → UI language**; once the user types, **first strong letter** wins. Do **not** use bare `dir="auto"` on fields (empty placeholders misalign in Chrome).

- [`components/Input.tsx`](components/Input.tsx): wraps field + optional `endAdornment` (password reveal) in one `dir={resolvedDir}` shell so inline-end controls stay opposite the text.
- [`AutoDirTextarea`](components/Input.tsx) for bare textareas; filter/settings inputs use the same resolver.
- Do **not** use [`getTextDirection`](utils/textDirection.ts) for inputs — that is for row layout when any RTL char appears anywhere.

## Exceptions (always LTR when filled)

Phone (`PhoneInput`, `type=tel`), email, URL, dates, API keys, webhooks, monospace secrets.

**Numbers:** use [`NumberInput`](components/NumberInput.tsx) or `Input type="number"`. Empty → UI language (Arabic placeholder aligns right); non-empty value → LTR. Do not force `dir="ltr"` on empty numeric fields.

## Related

- Display phones: `phone-rtl-display` rule (`PhoneText`), not inputs.
- Chat composers may compute direction explicitly; same first-letter semantics.
